  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nA heavily moisture ladened air mass dominates the region with CAM \nanalysis showing PWAT values around 2 inches across the Kansas and \nwestern Missouri River Valleys. A complex of showers and \nthunderstorms across central KS are expected to continue moving \neastward toward the region through the overnight. The 850mb LLJ is \nforecasted to expand northward through the overnight accelerating \nsoutherly flow across the low level. This combined with isentropic \nascent across the meso warm sector and a passing upper level \nshortwave results in precipitation expanding northward through the \novernight enveloping much of the western portions of the coverage \narea by sunrise. Meso-analystics continue to show a less than \nfavorable convective environment across a geographic scale with \nlimited instability, lowering bulk shear, and minimized lapse rates; \nhowever, model vertical profiles paint a slightly different picture. \nMost model soundings show a vertical plot of temperature and dew \npoint, in that the lines are more or less vertical. This is likely a \nresult of consistent warm air (and moisture) advection into the \nregion negating the low level effects of nocturnal cooling. However, \nthere is also a combination of enough warm air and moisture \nadvection across several layers of the atmosphere while also not \nbeing enough to create either surface instability or upper level \ncapping. This creates a profile with limited CAPE and ambient \nvertical motion. However, this also presents an environment that is \ncapable of being manipulated by relatively small factors. Rain \ncooled air aloft, an enhanced area of warm air below, for example \ncould alter the profile enough to keep convective storms going or \ndissipate them. Consensus leans towards CVA from the upper level \nwave tapping into the saturated vertical column creating just enough \nlift to generate some showers and isolated thunderstorms. The \nprimary uncertainty will be how the approaching thunderstorms \ncomplex to the west will manifest the environment. Likely we will \nsee a mixed combination of scattered showers and more robust \nthunderstorms. It is not likely that storms will alter the ambient \nenvironment significantly enough to create deep convection or \nmanifest severe hail or damaging winds, but with the high PWATs \nmentioned earlier, heavy rainfall remains the primary concern.\n\nRain moves in before sunrise Friday and sticks around through much \nof the daytime hours. Once rain moves out Friday night, ridging \nbegins to dominate the weather pattern once again bringing the \npotential for dangerous heat and humidity across the region. \nEnsemble guidance for Saturday shows a 60-80 percent chance of 105F+ \nheat indices south of the MO River. This is likely due in part to \nresident saturation from Friday's rainfall; so therein lies some \nuncertainty in peak heat potential as precipitation could affect the \ntemperature outlook. Sunday, the heat expands with 50-60 percent \nchances of 105F+ across the area and 30-40 percent chances of \nexceeding 110F. Looking into Monday and beyond becomes a little more \nuncertain as long range guidance favors centering the ridge over the \neastern Rockies which put our region right in the path of ridge \nrunning overnight thunderstorm complexes which are notorious for \nmessing with hazardous heat expectations. If storms do pass over, \nrain and residual cloud cover could taper heat; however, if not, \nhazardous heat becomes much more likely.",
